# Lecture 10D

## Chromosome Aneuploidy

## Chapter 9

## Dr. Neil Voss

## November 10, 2026

=== layout: bookend

# Learning Objectives

@body

- Distinguish [*polyploidy*]{color=#24578F} and [*aneuploidy*]{color=#935D00}
- Compare [*trisomy*]{color=#003FFF} and [*monosomy*]{color=#D40000}
- Predict gametes after [*nondisjunction*]{color=#24578F}
- Compare [*screening*]{color=#24578F} and [*diagnostic testing*]{color=#935D00}

=== layout: section

# Chromosome Aneuploidy

=== layout: one-panel

# *Chromosome Alterations*

@body

- Large-scale chromosomal alterations in humans and other mammals often lead to spontaneous abortions (miscarriages) or cause a variety of developmental disorders
- Plants tolerate such genetic changes better than animals do

=== layout: two-plus-one-panels


@top-left

- About 15% of all pregnancies terminate in spontaneous abortion.
- many (\~50%) due to chromosomal abnormalities

@bottom-left

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/monosomy_trisomy_effects.quote.svg)

@right

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_001.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# Chromosome Number and DNA Content

@body

{table-style=comparison}
| Question | Terms |
| --- | --- |
| Complete chromosome sets? | [*Euploid*]{color=#24578F}: whole sets; [*polyploid*]{color=#24578F}: more than two sets |
| Extra or missing individual chromosomes? | [*Aneuploid*]{color=#935D00} |
| DNA gained or lost in a rearrangement? | *Unbalanced*: gained/lost; *balanced*: neither |

[*Chromosome count*]{color=#24578F} and [*DNA content*]{color=#935D00}: separate questions

Chromosome rearrangements: December 1

=== layout: subsection

# Polyploid

=== layout: two-panels

# Polyploid: More Than Two Complete Sets

@left

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/ploidy_chromosome_multiplicity.drawn.svg)

@right

- *Diploid*: two complete sets
- *Triploid*: three complete sets
- *Tetraploid*: four complete sets
- Polyploidy is especially common in plants
- A polyploid may not produce fertile offspring with its diploid ancestor

=== layout: one-panel

# How Can a Cell Become Tetraploid?

@body

- [*Diploid cell*]{color=#24578F}: two complete sets
- DNA copying, then *incomplete cell division*
- Copies for [*both daughter cells*]{color=#935D00} retained

{table-style=comparison}
| Outcome | Chromosome Sets |
| --- | --- |
| Division finishes | Two daughter cells, two sets each |
| Division fails | One cell, four sets: [*tetraploid*]{color=#935D00} |

One human nucleus: [*46,XY*]{color=#24578F} -> [*92,XXYY*]{color=#935D00}

*DNA copying alone*: sister chromatids, [*still diploid*]{color=#24578F}

@citation

https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pgen.1002679

@notes

Follow the whole chromosome complement, not an extra copy of just one chromosome.
Failure of cell separation can initially leave two diploid nuclei in one cell. The
92,XXYY example describes a tetraploid complement in one nucleus, rather than every
possible failed division. A normal replicated diploid cell still has 46 chromosomes,
each with two sister chromatids; DNA replication alone does not change its ploidy.

=== layout: one-panel

# One Basic Set or One Gamete?

@body

{table-style=comparison column-widths="1.5,3"}
| Quantity | What Are We Counting? |
| --- | --- |
| [*Monoploid, m*]{color=#076DAD} | Chromosomes in one basic set |
| [*Haploid, h*]{color=#B74300} | Chromosomes in a gamete after regular reduction |

- Diploid parent: gamete with *one basic set*
- Octoploid parent: gamete with *four basic sets*
- Polyploids: [*m*]{color=#076DAD} and [*h*]{color=#B74300} can differ

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

An octoploid plant has *64 chromosomes* per somatic cell.

What are its [*monoploid (m)*]{color=#076DAD} and [*haploid (h)*]{color=#B74300} numbers? Assume regular reduction in meiosis.

- A. m = 8, h = 8
- B. m = 32, h = 32
- C. m = 8, h = 32
- D. m = 32, h = 8
- E. m = 64, h = 32
- F. m = 64, h = 8

@answer

C. m = 8, h = 32

=== layout: one-panel

# Count Sets First, Then Gametes

@body

{table-style=working}
| Quantity | Calculation | Answer |
| --- | --- | --- |
| One basic set | 64 &divide; 8 | [*m = 8*]{color=#076DAD} |
| One gamete | Half of 64 chromosomes | [*h = 32*]{color=#B74300} |

- [*Answer: C*]{color=#007A00}
- One gamete: *four basic sets*
- Homework notation *8n*: [*eight basic sets*]{color=#076DAD}

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

An octoploid plant has *72 chromosomes* in a somatic cell. How many chromosomes would be present in its gametes after regular reduction?

- A. 4
- B. 9
- C. 16
- D. 18
- E. 36

@answer

E. 36 chromosomes

=== layout: one-panel

# Gamete Count: Answer

@body

- [*Meiosis*]{color=#B74300}: chromosome count halved
- 72 &divide; 2 = [*36 chromosomes per gamete*]{color=#007A00}
- [*Answer: E*]{color=#007A00}
- [*One basic set*]{color=#076DAD}: 72 &divide; 8 = 9
- Four basic sets per gamete: 4 &times; 9 = 36

=== layout: subsection

# Aneuploid

=== layout: two-panels

# Extra or Missing Chromosomes

@left

- [*Trisomy*]{color=#003FFF}: three copies of one chromosome
- [*Monosomy*]{color=#D40000}: one copy of one chromosome
- [*Aneuploidy*]{color=#935D00}: individual chromosomes, not whole sets

@right

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/meiosis_ii_nondisjunction_lineage.drawn.svg)

=== layout: one-panel

# Find the Extra Chromosome

@body

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_005.png)

*Which chromosome has three copies?*

=== layout: one-panel

# Read the Extra Copy

@body

{table-style=comparison text-align=left}
| Karyotype Part | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| [*47*]{color=#D40000} | Total chromosome count |
| [*XY*]{color=#B74300} | Sex-chromosome complement |
| [*+21*]{color=#003FFF} | Extra copy of chromosome 21 |

Photograph's extra chromosome: [*+21*]{color=#003FFF} in *47,XY,+21*

DNA replication: more chromatids, *same chromosome count*

=== layout: subsection

# Nondisjunction

=== layout: one-panel

# What Failed to Separate?

@body

{table-style=comparison column-widths="1.5,2,2"}
| Division | Normal Separation | Nondisjunction |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [*Meiosis I*]{color=#24578F} | Homologous chromosomes | Homologs together in one cell |
| [*Meiosis II*]{color=#935D00} | Sister chromatids | Sisters together in one cell |

[*One chromosome pair*]{color=#24578F}; other chromosomes separating normally

=== layout: one-panel

# Nondisjunction in Meiosis I

@body

![Nondisjunction in Meiosis I](assets/lect10_repaired_figures/nondisjunction_meiosis_i.drawn.svg)

=== layout: one-panel

# Nondisjunction in Meiosis II

@body

![Nondisjunction in Meiosis II](assets/lect10_repaired_figures/nondisjunction_meiosis_ii.drawn.svg)

=== layout: one-panel

# Follow the Four Gametes

@body

![Follow the Four Gametes](assets/lect10_repaired_figures/nondisjunction_gametes.drawn.svg)

=== layout: one-panel

# Check the Four Products

@body

{table-style=comparison column-widths="1.6,2.6,2.3"}
| Error | Gametes | What Changed? |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [*Meiosis I*]{color=#24578F} | n + 1, n + 1, n - 1, n - 1 | All four abnormal |
| [*Meiosis II*]{color=#935D00} in one cell | n + 1, n - 1, n, n | Two normal |

Count [*all four products*]{color=#24578F} before choosing an answer

=== layout: one-panel

# Add a Wildtype Gamete

@body

{table-style=working}
| Gamete After an Error | Wildtype Gamete | Zygote | Result |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| [*n - 1*]{color=#D40000} | n | [*2n - 1*]{color=#D40000} | [*Monosomy*]{color=#D40000} |
| [*n + 1*]{color=#003FFF} | n | [*2n + 1*]{color=#003FFF} | [*Trisomy*]{color=#003FFF} |

- *Gamete*: n - 1 or n + 1
- *Diploid zygote*: 2n - 1 or 2n + 1
- For humans: 22 + 23 = 45; 24 + 23 = 47

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

Meiosis I is normal. Nondisjunction of one chromosome occurs in [*one daughter cell*]{color=#24578F} during *meiosis II*. What chromosome counts will the four gametes have?

- A) Four diploid gametes (2n).
- B) Two n + 1 and two n - 1.
- C) One n + 1, one n - 1, and two n.
- D) Two haploid (n) and two diploid (2n).

@answer

Answer\: C

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

*Try It: Follow the X Chromosome*

[*Father*]{color=#24578F}: X-linked recessive color blindness. [*Mother*]{color=#935D00}: two wildtype color-vision alleles. Daughter: color-blind, *45,X*.

Assume no new mutation or chromosome loss after fertilization.

Which parent produced the gamete missing a sex chromosome?

- A. Mother; X from father
- B. Father; X from mother
- C. Cannot be determined

@answer

A. Mother.

=== layout: one-panel

# Turner Syndrome: Follow the Allele

@body

- Daughter: *one X*, carrying the color-blindness allele
- [*Father*]{color=#24578F}: X-bearing sperm; source of the allele
- [*Mother*]{color=#935D00}: egg without an X
- [*Answer: A*]{color=#007A00}

Parent identified; meiotic stage *undetermined*

=== layout: subsection

# Autosomal Trisomies

=== layout: one-panel

# Three Common Autosomal Trisomies

@body

{table-style=comparison}
| Extra Chromosome | Syndrome | Examples of Effects |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [*13*]{color=#007A00 font-size=36pt text-align=center} | [*Patau*]{color=#007A00} | Brain and heart defects; cleft lip/palate |
| [*18*]{color=#935D00 font-size=36pt text-align=center} | [*Edwards*]{color=#935D00} | Low birthweight; organ defects; clenched fists |
| [*21*]{color=#003FFF font-size=36pt text-align=center} | [*Down*]{color=#003FFF} | Developmental delay; variable health effects |

- Trisomy *13 and 18*: often fatal early in life
- Trisomy *21*: survival into adulthood common

@citation

https://medlineplus.gov/genetics/condition/trisomy-13/  https://medlineplus.gov/genetics/condition/trisomy-18/

