=== layout: subsubsection

# Down Syndrome

=== layout: two-panels

# *Down Syndrome*

@left

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_008.png)

@right

- caused by the presence of an extra chromosome 21. In this case, instead of having two of chromosome 21, your baby will have three.
- characterized by delayed physical and mental development.

=== layout: two-panels

# Three Forms of Down Syndrome

@left

- *Trisomy 21*: an extra whole chromosome 21
- *Translocation*: extra chromosome 21 material attached elsewhere
- *Mosaicism*: extra chromosome 21 in only some cells

@right

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/down_syndrome_forms.drawn.svg)

@citation

https://www.cdc.gov/birth-defects/about/down-syndrome.html

=== layout: two-panels

# Trisomy 21 and Survival

@left

- Down syndrome is the most common chromosomal condition diagnosed in the United States
- Many people with Down syndrome live into adulthood
- Adult survival also occurs, rarely, with trisomy 13; it is not unique to trisomy 21

@right

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_010.png)

@citation

https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2325123/

=== layout: two-panels

# *Down Syndrome Stats*

@left

- Down syndrome occurs in people of all races and economic levels
- Most people with Down syndrome lead healthy lives, but they have an increased risk for certain medical conditions
  - such as congenital heart defects, respiratory and hearing problems, Alzheimer's disease, childhood leukemia and thyroid conditions.

@right

![Chromosome 21 trisomy karyotype beside a child with Down syndrome](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_013.jpg)

=== layout: two-panels

# *Down Syndrome Incidence*

@left

[*Read the graph:*]{color=#24578F}

- How does the frequency of Down syndrome change with maternal age?
- Compare ages *25, 35, and 45*
- Over which age range does the curve rise most steeply?

@right

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_011.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# Effect of Mother's Age

@left

- The chance of trisomy 21 increases with maternal age
- Eggs begin meiosis before birth, then pause for many years
- Many children with Down syndrome are born to younger mothers because more births occur in that age group

@right

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_012.png)

@citation

https://www.cdc.gov/birth-defects/about/down-syndrome.html

=== layout: two-panels

# Why Is Age a Factor?

@left

[*More cohesin*]{color=#935D00}

![Two blue sister chromatids held close together by four orange cohesin rings](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/cohesion_maintained.drawn.svg)

- Sister chromatids held together

@right

[*Less cohesin*]{color=#935D00}

![Two blue sister chromatids with fewer orange cohesin rings and loosened arms](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/cohesion_weakened.drawn.svg)

- Weaker connections
- More segregation errors

@citation

Simplified schematic. Age-related cohesin loss in mouse and human eggs: https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0096710

@notes

Blue: sister chromatids of one replicated chromosome. Orange rings: cohesin complexes,
shown schematically, not to scale. Both panels show the same chromosome identity and DNA amount.
The changed spacing illustrates weakened cohesion, not a measured distance or a specific age.
Arm cohesion helps maintain chiasmata between homologs; centromeric cohesion keeps sisters
associated. Loss can disturb homolog segregation or permit premature sister separation.
Tsutsumi et al. (2014) measured age-related loss of meiosis-specific cohesin subunits in
human and mouse oocytes. Cohesion deterioration is one contributor to maternal-age effects.

=== layout: question-ask

# Can People with Down Syndrome Have Children?

=== layout: two-panels

# Down Syndrome and Fertility

@left

[*Men: commonly believed to be sterile*]{color=#24578F}

- As of 2005: *four pregnancies* fathered by *three men* with Down syndrome
- Defective spermatogenesis
- How common is male fertility? *Still uncertain*

@right

[*Women can and do have children*]{color=#935D00}

- What is the likelihood that their offspring will have Down syndrome?

@citation

Historical reports: Pradhan et al. (2006), https://doi.org/10.1016/j.fertnstert.2006.03.071

@notes

Restore the original slide's teaching contrast and historical counts, not a current census.
The original phrasing was: "Men with Down syndrome are commonly believed to be sterile."
The 2006 case report documents paternity in a man with nonmosaic Down syndrome.
Rare documented paternity establishes that male infertility is not absolute; population
fertility and paternal transmission risk remain poorly quantified. Do not describe all men
as sterile or male fertility as completely unknown. See also the 2015 primary case report:
https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4595396/
The offspring question leads into the explicit chromosome-segregation model on the next slide.

=== layout: one-panel

# A Simplified Segregation Problem

@body

- Trisomic cell: [*two equally common gamete types*]{color=#24578F}
  - One copy of chromosome 21
  - Two copies of chromosome 21
- Wildtype gamete: *one copy*
- Model assumptions: [*equal fertilization and survival*]{color=#935D00}

*What fraction of the resulting zygotes would have trisomy 21?*

*Chromosome model; not live-birth risk*

