# How Three Homologs Separate during Meiosis

=== layout: one-panel


@body

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/trisomic_metaphase_arrangements.drawn.svg)

=== layout: one-panel

# Two Copies Go One Way, One the Other

@body

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/trisomic_metaphase_anaphase.drawn.svg)

=== layout: one-panel


@body

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/trisomic_anaphase_products.drawn.svg)

=== layout: two-panels

# The Model Gives a 1:1 Ratio

@left

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/trisomic_meiosis_gamete_products.drawn.svg)

@right

- Gametes: half with [*one copy*]{color=#24578F}, half with [*two*]{color=#935D00}
- Wildtype gamete: *one more copy*
- Zygotes: half with two copies, half with three
- [*Model answer: 50% trisomy*]{color=#007A00}

Actual outcomes: additional effects of *fertilization and survival*

=== layout: subsection

# Prenatal Testing

=== layout: one-panel

# Screening for Down Syndrome

@body

- Start with a *maternal blood test* or *ultrasound*
- Estimate the chance of [*Down syndrome*]{color=#003FFF}
- No needle enters the uterus for these screening tests
- A positive screen is *not a diagnosis*

@citation

https://www.nichd.nih.gov/health/topics/down/conditioninfo/diagnosis

=== layout: one-panel

# Down Syndrome: Triple Screen

@body

- *Maternal blood test*: AFP, hCG, and estriol
- Low AFP/estriol and high hCG can suggest [*Down syndrome*]{color=#003FFF}
- *Second trimester*; a quad screen adds inhibin-A
- Estimates chance; does not count fetal chromosomes

@citation

https://www.testing.com/tests/maternal-serum-screening-second-trimester/

@notes

Keep the question anchored to trisomy 21: does this marker pattern suggest an increased chance?
The triple/quad screen is not the first-trimester blood panel used with the NT scan.
These are established serum-screening examples; cell-free DNA is another screening option.

=== layout: one-plus-two-panels

# Down Syndrome: Nuchal Translucency

@left

- *Ultrasound*: fluid behind the fetal neck
- Increased NT can suggest [*Down syndrome*]{color=#003FFF}
- First trimester: about *11-14 weeks*
- Combined with first-trimester blood markers
- Estimates chance; does not confirm trisomy 21

@top-right

![Ultrasound measuring nuchal translucency](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_017.png)

@bottom-right

![Ultrasound showing the nuchal translucency region](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_018.png)

@citation

https://www.nichd.nih.gov/health/topics/down/conditioninfo/diagnosis

@notes

Both ultrasound images illustrate NT measurement, not a karyotype diagnosis.
Increased NT is not specific to Down syndrome. Do not label an image as diagnostic by appearance.
First-trimester blood markers are PAPP-A and hCG, not the second-trimester triple screen.

=== layout: one-panel

# Screening Can Be Wrong

@body

{table-style=comparison column-widths="1.3,2,2"}
| Result | Screen Says | Actual Chromosomes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [*False positive*]{color=#935D00} | Higher chance of Down syndrome | Wildtype: two copies of 21 |
| [*False negative*]{color=#24578F} | Lower chance of Down syndrome | Trisomy 21: three copies |

- A *positive* result does not prove Down syndrome
- A *negative* result does not rule it out

@citation

https://www.cdc.gov/birth-defects/diagnosis/screening-for-birth-defects.html

@notes

For this comparison, wildtype means two copies of chromosome 21; it is not a judgment about people.
Ask students which error could lead to unnecessary worry and which could miss a pregnancy
with trisomy 21. Both serum screening and NT screening can produce either kind of error.

=== layout: one-panel

# A Positive Screen: What Next?

@body

- Offer genetic counseling and *diagnostic testing*
- Sample cells for a [*karyotype*]{color=#935D00}: check chromosome 21

{table-style=comparison}
| Screening | Diagnostic Testing |
| --- | --- |
| Blood tests, ultrasound | Sample cells using CVS or amniocentesis |
| Chance of Down syndrome | Karyotype can show extra chromosome 21 |

- Sampling is *invasive* and carries a risk of miscarriage
- Parents may accept or decline further testing

@citation

https://www.nichd.nih.gov/health/topics/down/conditioninfo/diagnosis

@notes

The teaching sequence follows a positive screen into confirmation. A positive screen is not
required to choose diagnostic testing; patients can also choose it directly after counseling.
CVS and amniocentesis are sampling procedures. The laboratory karyotype examines chromosomes.

=== layout: one-panel

# Invasive Testing: Getting Cells

@body

{table-style=comparison}
| Sample | Where It Comes From |
| --- | --- |
| [*Amniocentesis*]{color=#D40000} | Amniotic fluid containing fetal cells |
| [*Chorionic Villus Sampling (CVS)*]{color=#007A00} | Placental tissue |
| [*Umbilical Blood Sampling (PUBS)*]{color=#B74300} | Fetal blood from the umbilical cord |

Cells -> [*karyotype*]{color=#935D00} -> extra chromosome 21?

