=== layout: one-panel

# Parental Choice or Eugenics?

@body

- How do we protect parents' reproductive choices?
- When could individual choices become social pressure?
- How does support for people with Down syndrome affect those choices?
- What distinguishes personal choice from eugenics?

@notes

Pause for discussion before advancing to the testing-market material.
Allow quiet thinking, partner discussion, then several viewpoints. No personal disclosure is needed.
Ask students to distinguish reproductive autonomy, coercion, discrimination, and population goals.
Avoid assuming that every decision after a diagnosis is the same or has the same motivation.

=== layout: two-panels

# Prenatal Testing: Testing Too Much?

@left

- NYTimes investigation: screening for *rare disorders*
- Many positive results were *false positives*
- Does more testing always mean better information?

@right

![Original infographic comparing correct and false-positive rare-condition screening results](assets/lect10d-chromo_aneuploid/image_031.png)

@citation

NYTimes: [January 2022 investigation](https://www.nytimes.com/2022/01/01/upshot/pregnancy-birth-genetic-testing.html); [April 2022 follow-up](https://www.nytimes.com/2022/04/20/upshot/prenatal-genetic-tests-warning.html).

@notes

The investigation concerns expanded screening for rare conditions. Do not apply its
false-positive proportions to Down syndrome screening or to diagnostic karyotypes.
Return to the denominator: among positive screens, how many were confirmed?

